// src/kernel/boot/witness/r86_relative_path.pdx — R86.M1-008 (paideia-os #1961)
//
// Dormant symbol-linkage scaffold for the R86 relative-path substrate
// (TASK_OFF_CWD, sys_chdir sysno 85, sys_getcwd sysno 86, the cwd-threaded
// sys_open/sys_stat/sys_mkdir/sys_rmdir/sys_unlink/sys_rename, and the
// vnode name table that backs sys_getcwd's parent-chain walk).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this witness proves
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   (A) `r86_relative_path_witness` links into build/kernel.elf and is
//       reachable as a `call r86_relative_path_witness` from
//       kernel_main.pdx's boot_continue_after_ring3 (wired unconditionally
//       — see the call site there for the exact placement, last in the
//       ring-0 witness chain, right before the release-ready line).
//   (B) Nothing else. The body is a symbol-existence scaffold that returns
//       86 (SKIP sentinel) without invoking sys_chdir_body, sys_getcwd_body,
//       or sys_mkdir_body.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the body is deferred — the SAME structural limitation R56.M3-006
// documented for the sibling VFS-metadata syscalls
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sys_chdir_body walks its path argument via user_read_str_via_walk;
// sys_getcwd_body validates its destination buffer via user_ptr_ok and
// copies out via user_write_bytes_via_walk. Both walkers resolve the
// caller-supplied VA against the CURRENT task's real page tables. A
// kernel-side call site at this point in boot — before the ring-3 hand-off,
// with _current_tcb either unset or pointing at init's not-yet-executing
// TCB — hands the walker a .bss scratch VA that either misses the mapping
// (walker returns 0/1 -> -EFAULT) or, worse, faults on a kernel-only page.
// This is byte-for-byte the same limitation
// src/kernel/boot/witness/r56_meta.pdx's own header documents for
// sys_mkdir_body / sys_stat_body / sys_rmdir_body / sys_unlink_body /
// sys_rename_body / sys_getdents_body — R86's new sys_chdir_body and
// sys_getcwd_body inherit it because they were deliberately built
// self-contained, matching sys_stat_body's own shape (per the R86.M1-002/
// 003 task pin), rather than the older sys_open/sys_read/sys_write shape
// where the walker lives at the DISPATCH layer and the body takes a
// kernel VA directly (see dispatch.pdx's "R16-M3-KPTI-sweep" header for
// that split's own history).
//
// Making this witness invoke the three bodies from ring 0 therefore
// requires the same two options R56.M3-006 named and deferred:
//
//   (i)  Forging a synthetic user aspace (dedicated PML4 + user-VA mapping
//        of a scratch page) around the call sequence, restoring CR3 on
//        exit; OR
//   (ii) Driving the syscalls through a REAL ring-3 task that already has
//        a real user aspace.
//
// R86 takes (ii), but NOT as a kernel boot witness: tools/run-smoke.sh's
// `boot_r86_relative_path` mode injects an actual command script (`mkdir
// /tmp`, `cd /tmp`, `mkdir ./sub`, `cd ./sub`, `mkdir ../peer`, `pwd`,
// `exit`) into the REAL interactive shell (src/user/shell.pdx +
// dispatch.pdx's cd_builtin/pwd_builtin, R86.M1-006/007 #1959/#1960) via
// QEMU's serial console, and diffs the resulting log against
// tests/expected-r86-relative-path.golden — mirroring the existing
// boot_r17_shell_multi_command precedent (which already exercises `cd` +
// `pwd` this same way, just without the mkdir/getcwd depth this round
// adds). That mechanism sidesteps the walker limitation entirely because
// the shell IS a real ring-3 task with a real user aspace; it needs no
// kernel-side witness file to do it, exactly as boot_r17_shell_multi_command
// needs none of its own.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fingerprint contract (for a future synthetic-aspace kernel-side
// exercise, if one is ever built)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// tests/expected-r86-relative-path.golden documents the fingerprint
// sequence a full ring-3 run of the script above produces, in order:
//
//   sys mkdir ok [legacy: SYS MKDIR OK]        (mkdir /tmp)
//   shell cd ok -- path=/tmp                    (cd /tmp)
//   sys mkdir ok [legacy: SYS MKDIR OK]        (mkdir ./sub)
//   shell cd ok -- path=/tmp/sub                (cd ./sub)
//   sys mkdir ok [legacy: SYS MKDIR OK]        (mkdir ../peer)
//   /tmp/sub                                    (pwd, literal ASCII via
//                                                sys_write of the
//                                                sys_getcwd-composed path)
//
// Each fingerprint is emitted by its own body's own klog_s1_x1 tail (the
// two `sys mkdir ok` lines) or by cd_builtin/pwd_builtin's own
// sys_debug_puts/sys_write calls (the `shell cd ok --` and literal path
// lines) — this witness never emits its own per-primitive fingerprint.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Wire posture at R86.M1
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WIRED (unconditionally) into kernel_main.pdx's boot_continue_after_ring3,
// unlike r56_meta_witness which is deliberately left unwired. The
// difference is safe because this body is a plain immediate-return SKIP
// scaffold with zero side effects (no memory writes, no nested calls) —
// wiring it costs one `call` + `ret` at boot and satisfies the R86.M1-009
// (paideia-os #1962) requirement that a witness exist at the call site the
// round's own PAIDEIA_R86_CWD=1 pre-push gate can point at, mirroring the
// R68.M1-005 PAIDEIA_R68_DISK=1 gate's own "R56 leg: boot_r56_meta is a
// dormant SKIP-echo scaffold" posture (.githooks/pre-push).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reserved-label discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Labels prefixed `r86rp_` throughout. Grep audit 2026-08-25: no `r86rp_`
// hits anywhere else in src/kernel/.

module R86RelativePath = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // r86_relative_path_witness() -> u64
  //
  //   Returns 86 (SKIP sentinel — the sysno this round's mutator,
  //   sys_chdir, claims) at R86.M1-008. The body is a scaffold — it does
  //   not touch sys_chdir_body / sys_getcwd_body / sys_mkdir_body. See
  //   the module header's "Why the body is deferred" section for the
  //   full rationale.
  //
  //   Alignment: entry rsp%16==8 (SysV, called from a leaf `call` site
  //   in kernel_main.pdx's boot_continue_after_ring3). One push (rbp)
  //   lands rsp%16==0 (aligned for any future nested call added if this
  //   ever grows a real synthetic-aspace exercise). Matching pop
  //   restores rbp for the caller.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let r86_relative_path_witness : () -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: { sysreg, mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "R86.M1-008 (#1961). Dormant symbol-linkage scaffold for the relative-path substrate (TASK_OFF_CWD, sys_chdir sysno 85, sys_getcwd sysno 86). Symbol-existence witness: links into build/kernel.elf, wired unconditionally into kernel_main.pdx's boot_continue_after_ring3 (last in the ring-0 witness chain), and returns 86 (SKIP sentinel) without invoking sys_chdir_body/sys_getcwd_body/sys_mkdir_body. Real invocation is deferred to the REAL ring-3 shell — tools/run-smoke.sh's boot_r86_relative_path mode drives an actual command script through the actual shell binary and diffs the resulting serial log against tests/expected-r86-relative-path.golden — because sys_chdir_body / sys_getcwd_body resolve their path/buffer arguments via user_read_str_via_walk / user_ptr_ok / user_write_bytes_via_walk, which validate the caller-supplied VA against the CURRENT task's real page tables; a kernel-side call site at this point in boot has no such task, exactly the limitation src/kernel/boot/witness/r56_meta.pdx's own header documents for the sibling R56.M3 VFS-metadata syscall bodies (mkdir/rmdir/stat/getdents/unlink/rename). Wiring this dormant scaffold in (rather than leaving it unwired like r56_meta_witness) is safe because the body has zero side effects — one push, one immediate load, one pop, one ret — and it satisfies the R86.M1-009 (#1962) requirement that PAIDEIA_R86_CWD=1's pre-push gate have a wired call site to point at, mirroring PAIDEIA_R68_DISK=1's own dormant-scaffold posture for its R56 leg. Body: 1-push (rbp) callee-save prologue lands rsp%16==0 (entry %16==8 + 8 = %16==0), aligned for any future nested call a synthetic-aspace exercise would add. Immediate `mov rax, 86; pop rbp; ret`. Matches r56_meta_witness / r54_bdev_flush_witness / r55_write_e2e_witness dormant-scaffold precedent. Reserved labels: r86rp_ (none used — the body has no branches).",
      block: {
        push rbp;
        mov rax, 86;
        pop rbp;
        ret
      }
    }
}
